{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "אשר לא צעקה", :translit "asher lo tze'akah", :gloss "who did not cry out"} {:he "ואף אם יראה ממנו", :translit "ve'af im yareiah mimenno", :gloss "and even if she was afraid of him"} {:he "היתה יכולה לומר", :translit "hayetah yekholah lomar", :gloss "she could have said"} {:he "מחמת צער בתולים", :translit "mikhmat tza'ar betulim", :gloss "on account of the pain of virginity"} {:he "צועקת", :translit "tzo'eket", :gloss "she cries out"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Legal argument: a plausible pretext for crying out exists, so silence implies consent."}], :frame {:text "The Torah's law of the betrothed girl (na'arah ha-m'orasah) in Deuteronomy 22:23-27 distinguishes between a town, where the girl is presumed able to cry out for help, and a field, where she is not. The Gemara at Ketubot 51b takes up cases where her silence might be excused—e.g., if she was forcibly silenced—and debates whether fear or physical inability removes the obligation to resist audibly. Ba'al HaTurim applies that reasoning here: even genuine terror would not fully explain her silence, since the pain itself supplies both motive and cover for screaming.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 22:23-27" "Ketubot 51b"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Legal argument: a plausible pretext for crying out exists, so silence implies consent.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:38:30.040668961Z", :model "glm-5.2"}